const EXPLAIN = {
  "A6": "The correct answer is equipment grounding, because a tingling sensation while mounting a server in metal racks means fault current is traveling through the technician instead of a bonded ground path. Chassis and data-center racks must be connected to electrical ground so leakage or a short is redirected away from people, and ground prongs on power plugs must never be removed. Air filtration, cable management, and waste-disposal rules are real data-center concerns, but none of them explain or stop an electrical tingle during rack installation.",
  "A7": "Enabling auto-lock would have the best impact on battery life in this scenario. The phone still reports 100 percent battery health, yet auto-lock is disabled, so the display never sleeps and the case runs warm after the device joined the corporate network and stayed on Wi-Fi all day. Connecting to a VPN would add overhead rather than save power, storage is only about half used so freeing space would not help, and turning off Wi-Fi would break the corporate connectivity the phone now needs.",
  "A8": "An MDM policy restriction is the most likely reason the app will not install on the new corporate phone. Mobile device management can block unapproved store apps or sideloading, so the install fails every time rather than hanging or running slowly. File-system type is not something a user chooses when installing a mobile app, and a slow CPU or low battery might delay a download but would not reliably reject the installation.",
  "A9": "An integrated camera is required because FRT is facial recognition technology that authenticates from the user's face. The device builds a digital key from facial features, often with an infrared camera and a 3-D map, so a camera on the laptop, phone, or tablet is mandatory. A fingerprint reader is a different biometric, a badge reader checks a proximity card, and a token generator produces rotating codes; none of those capture a face.",
  "A10": "An air filter mask is the most important item because the technician is cleaning computers that sit in sawdust and wood chips. Fine airborne dust should not be inhaled during maintenance, so PPE must match that dust hazard rather than a chemical or electrical one. A surge suppressor and temperature sensor protect equipment, not the technician's lungs, and an ESD mat is for static control when handling internals, not for wiping enclosed dusty cases.",
  "A11": "Clearing the browser cache is the best way to fix a site that becomes slow and fails to display some items after a successful login. Locally stored images, scripts, and cookies can go stale and no longer match what the site is sending, which produces partial pages and sluggish rendering. Disabling notifications does not repair broken page assets, updating a web-server certificate is a trust issue that usually shows a warning instead of missing content, and a BIOS update has nothing to do with how a browser paints a page.",
  "A12": "A carbon dioxide extinguisher is the best way to put out flames inside a smoking server. Electrical Class C fires on electronic equipment should be fought with CO2, dry chemical, or FM-200, not water or foam, which can conduct current and damage remaining hardware. A water hose is dangerous on live gear, foam leaves residue and can still conduct, and disconnecting power is wise if it is safe but does not itself extinguish a fire that is already visible.",
  "A13": "BitLocker is the Windows feature that provides full-disk encryption for all data on a storage drive. It encrypts the entire volume so data at rest cannot be read if the drive is removed or stolen. EFS encrypts individual files or folders rather than the whole disk, Domain Services is Active Directory infrastructure, and RDP is remote desktop access, so none of those are full-disk encryption.",
  "A14": "This data is PII because customer names, addresses, and phone numbers can identify a person on their own or when combined, which is why a five-year retention policy applies. Personally identifiable information must be handled under strict rules to prevent unauthorized access. Credit-card transactions are PCI payment data, government-issued information would be items such as a passport or driver's license number, and healthcare data is PHI; none of those match the fields listed.",
  "A15": "Remote backup provides this functionality because a copy stored off-site or in the cloud can be restored to a replacement smartphone after damage or theft. Off-site backups survive local loss and can be pulled onto a new device quickly once it is set up. Full-device encryption protects data on the lost phone but does not put files on the replacement, IoT isolation is a network-segmentation control, and remote wipe erases the old device instead of restoring it.",
  "A16": "That page is a splash screen, the pre-login banner that shows disclaimers and usage requirements before the login prompt. Corporate workstations and portals use these banners so the user acknowledges monitoring and acceptable-use terms before access is granted. An acceptable use policy is the written rule set those banners summarize, standard operating procedures are how-to documents, and a topology diagram maps the network; none of those are the interstitial page itself.",
  "A17": "User Accounts is the Control Panel location that exposes the UAC settings the administrator needs to change for the older application. From there you can manage local accounts and adjust how User Account Control prompts. Device Manager lists hardware, System Information reports configuration, and Event Viewer shows logs, so none of them modify the UAC process.",
  "A18": "Connecting a surge suppressor to each system is the best way to handle occasional voltage spikes during the business day. The suppressor absorbs the extra energy and dumps it to ground so the PSU and motherboard do not take the hit. Powering systems down when idle does nothing during those daytime spikes, confirming a building ground is good practice but does not clamp a spike at the outlet, and stocking spare power supplies only helps after a PSU has already failed.",
  "A19": "A 32-bit operating system supports a maximum of 4 GB of RAM. A 32-bit address space tops out at 2 to the 32nd bytes, so that architectural cap applies even if the motherboard has more memory installed. 8 GB, 16 GB, and 192 GB are figures associated with 64-bit Windows editions, not with a 32-bit OS.",
  "A20": "Event Viewer is the tool that provides details about each previous application crash. Windows records application faults in the event logs, so the technician can review timestamps and error data from earlier failures instead of watching only the current session. Task Manager shows processes that are running now, and Startup Repair or Safe Mode are recovery options rather than a history of past crashes.",
  "A21": "This is a brute force attack because the attacker systematically tries every combination of letters, numbers, and special characters until the password is found. That exhaustive search is the definition of brute force rather than a trick or a flood. Spoofing impersonates a host or address, social engineering targets people, and a DDoS overwhelms a service to take it offline.",
  "A22": "The administrator should follow the rollback plan to return the email server to its previous state after the failed upgrade. A rollback is documented before the change and may include restoring a snapshot or otherwise undoing the install when repeated attempts keep failing. A disaster recovery plan covers site-wide outages, an incident response plan is for security events, and a power plan is a Windows energy setting, not a change-control undo.",
  "A23": "Moving the webcam to a different USB interface is the best next step because the controller has run out of USB endpoints, not system RAM. Another root hub or port often has spare resources so the device can enumerate without the resource warning. Closing large-memory processes does not free USB controller endpoints, editing the BCD changes boot options, and System Restore is a broad rollback when a simple port change should be tried first.",
  "A24": "Technicians will most likely map the shared folder to an available drive letter so the OS-image share appears as a local path in File Explorer. Windows commonly mounts a server share this way from This PC or with net use against the UNC path. A proxy is for web traffic, linking the images to cloud storage is unnecessary when the share already exists on the LAN, and a remote access client is for interactive sessions rather than pulling installation files from a mapped drive.",
  "A25": "ReFS is the best file-system choice for installing Windows Server. The Resilient File System is Microsoft's design for large volumes and storage arrays, with integrity checking and self-repair on Server 2012 and later. APFS is Apple's file system, and ext4 and XFS are Linux file systems, so they are not the native choice on a Windows Server install.",
  "A26": "The malware is a rootkit because it is running in the OS kernel and traditional anti-malware scans cannot see it. Rootkits take privileged system-level access and hide their processes and files from user-mode detection tools. A worm spreads copies of itself, a botnet is a collection of remotely controlled hosts, and a cryptominer burns CPU or GPU to mine currency; those do not specifically hide inside the kernel from scanners.",
  "A27": "Power-cycling the tablets is the best next troubleshooting step when the training app shows a splash screen and then exits on every device. Restarting clears a stuck process or bad in-memory state and is the least invasive action after a mobile app crash. Installing all operating-system updates, uninstalling the application, or rolling back a version are later steps once a simple reboot has been ruled out.",
  "A28": "A slow profile load is the most likely reason because the user authenticates normally and only then waits fifteen minutes for the desktop, which appeared in seconds yesterday. That pattern matches a roaming or domain profile that is taking a long time to sync over the network. An incorrect boot device would stop Windows from starting, faulty RAM typically causes crashes or instability rather than a delayed desktop, and a wrong username or password would fail the login itself.",
  "A29": "The issue is OS compatibility: a 32-bit operating system cannot run a 64-bit application. The 64-bit binary uses a pointer size and instruction set the 32-bit kernel does not support, so the install fails regardless of rights or free space. File permissions, the installation method, and available drive space do not create that architecture mismatch.",
  "A30": "These actions are tailgating, also called piggybacking, because the intruder followed an authorized person through a badge-controlled door without presenting credentials. The attacker relies on the first person to open a restricted entry and slips through before the door closes, bypassing the badge check. Dumpster diving is searching trash for information, brute force is guessing passwords, and phishing is a fraudulent message meant to steal data or access.",
  "A31": "nano is the utility that can modify the Linux service configuration text file. It is a terminal-based editor used for quick configuration changes without complex modal commands. chmod changes file permissions, df reports disk space, and sudo elevates a command; none of those open and edit the file's contents.",
  "A32": "A boot sector virus is most likely because the malware is not stored as a normal file in the OS and it runs every time the system starts. It infects the master boot record or partition boot sector and loads before the operating system, which is why file-based scans can miss it. A Trojan hides inside a program the user runs, ransomware encrypts files for payment, and a keylogger or spyware sits in the OS to capture input or activity.",
  "A33": "The best way to handle this is to repeat your understanding of the issue and give an estimate plus a follow-up time. That confirms you heard the real problem, shows active listening, and sets a clear timeline instead of arguing about the customer's suggested repair. Refusing the repair until the user calms down or lecturing them about a mistaken request escalates the situation, and refusing any commitment until the exam is done leaves an upset customer without a next step.",
  "A34": "After the factory reset the user should verify the source of each app before installing again. The flood of advertisements after loading productivity apps is typical of malware sideloaded from unofficial stores or APK files, so only trusted stores such as Google Play should be used. Disabling Bluetooth, checking app-sharing permissions, and running a cellular speed test do not stop ad-injecting apps from being put back on the phone.",
  "A35": "This process is just-in-time access because a temporary administrator password is issued only for the duration of the change and then deleted automatically. Elevated rights last only as long as the task requires, which avoids standing admin credentials that linger afterward. Single sign-on lets one login open many apps, data loss prevention watches for sensitive data leaving the network, and User Access Console is not the CompTIA term for time-limited privileged credentials.",
  "A36": "The hardware device is used for MFA because the user already has a username and password and must also enter a rotating six-digit code from something they physically possess. That second factor is a hardware token that changes the code about every 30 seconds. An ACL filters traffic or permissions, Group Policy configures Windows, SMS would send a code by text rather than display it on a token, and least privilege is about limiting rights rather than proving identity with two factors.",
  "A37": "Task Manager is the utility that helps troubleshoot the fifteen-minute startup after several applications were installed. Its Startup apps list shows which programs launch at sign-in and how much impact they have, so high-impact entries can be disabled. Defrag works on disks, Performance Monitor graphs counters over time, and robocopy copies files; none of them manage which applications start with Windows.",
  "A38": "The first step is to disconnect all power sources before opening the database server to replace memory. Unplugging the system and discharging standby power prevents shock while modules are handled. Removing the existing DIMMs comes after the machine is safe to touch, an air filter mask is for dust or toner rather than a RAM swap, and an ESD strap matters for static control but does not replace cutting power first.",
  "A39": "A regular format is the best way to reuse the lab computers while making the previous project's data unrecoverable. A regular format overwrites every sector with zeros so files cannot be restored, which has been the Windows default since Vista. A quick format only clears the file table, degaussing magnetically ruins the media and is not appropriate if the drives must be used again, and reinstalling the operating system leaves leftover data in unused space.",
  "A40": "The command to make the logs folder the current directory is cd logs. cd changes the working directory so the administrator can then open the application log files in that folder. mv logs would rename or move the folder, dir logs would list its contents, and md logs would create a new folder named logs.",
  "A41": "GPT is the best partition style for eight bootable partitions on a single SSD. A GUID partition table supports up to 128 partitions and very large volumes on UEFI systems without needing extended partitions or logical drives. MBR is limited to four primary partitions, NTFS is a file system rather than a partition table, and diskpart is the tool used to create partitions, not the style itself.",
  "A42": "Loading third-party drivers is the way to continue when Windows Setup does not recognize the drive controller. RAID, NVMe, and some chipset storage controllers need a vendor driver during install so the disk becomes visible. Restarting will not add that driver, a remote network installation still has to talk to the same unseen controller, and a recovery partition is not available on a machine that does not yet have an operating system.",
  "A43": "Using secure DNS would prevent this reconnaissance because ordinary DNS queries are readable in a packet capture and reveal the FQDNs users resolve. DNS over HTTPS encrypts those lookups so an observer cannot monitor which sites are requested. Full-disk encryption protects data at rest, multifactor authentication strengthens logins, and Secure Boot checks boot files; none of those hide DNS lookups on the wire.",
  "A44": "The smartphone storage is nearly full, which is the most likely reason the OS update will not start. The installer needs free space to download and stage the new build, so a packed device simply refuses to begin. Bluetooth and rotation lock do not block updates, and being connected to a power source is usually required for an upgrade, not a reason it fails.",
  "A45": "Time Machine should be used to restore the data onto the new drive in the macOS desktop. It is the built-in macOS backup feature that keeps hourly, daily, and weekly snapshots that can be applied after a disk replacement. Backup and Restore is a Windows tool, Mission Control manages spaces and windows, and Disk Utility can erase or repair a disk but does not restore the Time Machine history.",
  "A46": "The tax software was removed because the user installed a personal license on a company computer. A home or individual license is not valid for business deployment, so corporate software control can strip it after a policy scan. Exhausted per-seat licenses would typically block a new activation rather than silently uninstall a working copy, an open-source license would not trigger removal, and a USB DRM key would prevent launch rather than auto-delete the program.",
  "A47": "A clean install is the best way to move these Windows 8.1 workstations to Windows 11 because no local documents or settings need to be kept. Wiping and installing fresh avoids carrying forward the old OS when user files and applications already live on the server. A factory reset is aimed at restoring vendor defaults, a repair installation keeps the current OS, and an in-place upgrade is for when you must preserve local apps and settings.",
  "A48": "Disconnecting the network cable is the best next step because the manufacturing PC has already been identified as malware-infected and must be quarantined. Isolating Ethernet immediately helps stop lateral movement, and files should not be copied off the machine. Scanning or downloading new anti-malware signatures while still on the LAN risks spreading the infection, and disabling System Restore is a later removal step after the system is isolated.",
  "A49": "The technician's best next step is to inform the user of the repair status now that a week-long third-party hardware wait is known. Long tickets need a clear timeline so the customer is not left guessing while the printer is down. Moving to the next ticket or setting a follow-up reminder without telling the user leaves them uninformed, and ordering a printer maintenance kit does not address a part that a vendor must replace.",
  "A50": "The best next troubleshooting step is to modify the service permissions, because a repeating message that one or more services failed to start points to rights, a service account, or a dependency, not a hardware fault. Windows services stay offline until those configuration issues are corrected. Checking cooling or running a hardware diagnostic fits crashes and thermal shutdowns, and rebuilding the BCD is for boot-loader errors, not services that fail after Windows is already starting.",
  "A51": "Task Scheduler is the best way to make help desk PCs check for anti-virus signatures every hour during the workday. Task Scheduler (taskschd.msc) runs programs, scripts, or tasks at set times, intervals, or other triggers, so an hourly update job can be limited to business hours. System Configuration is for startup and boot options, not recurring jobs. Device Manager manages hardware, Certificate Manager stores certificates, and SFC repairs Windows system files, none of which schedule signature updates.",
  "A52": "Disable the startup applications, because extra programs launching at sign-in are a common reason a manufacturing-floor workstation suddenly takes much longer to boot. Startup Apps in Task Manager or Settings shows user-space programs that run at logon and rates their startup impact, and turning off high-impact items is a standard first fix. Replacing the CPU or adding RAM is hardware work without a hardware fault. Installing the latest OS patches may help other problems, but it is not the targeted step for a slow boot caused by startup programs.",
  "A53": "Administrator rights are required because the new application also installs a Windows service. An Administrator account has full control to install software, change system configuration, and add services that run for the whole computer. A Guest account has almost no privileges, and a standard user can install some per-user apps but cannot install a service. Power User is a leftover group from older Windows and does not grant the rights needed to install a service on current systems.",
  "A54": "A disconnected VPN is the most likely reason a home user cannot print to a laser printer at the office. A VPN builds an encrypted tunnel over the public Internet so a remote PC can reach private office resources such as printers; if that tunnel is down, the home computer is not on the corporate network. WPA3 settings only affect local wireless security. Outdated anti-virus signatures would not typically hide one office printer. MDM configuration applies to managed mobile devices, not this home-to-office print path.",
  "A55": "Share permissions restricting access from remote devices explain why Everyone has NTFS Read but users on other computers still cannot open the folder. Share permissions apply only to network connections and sit in front of NTFS; the effective right is the more restrictive of the two, so a tight share ACL blocks remote users even when NTFS is open. NTFS permissions do not need to be synchronized across PCs. Being an Administrator would increase access, not deny it, and remote users are not automatically Guest accounts.",
  "A56": "Shred the drives, because the healthcare administrator must guarantee that nothing on the old desktops can be recovered after recycling. Industrial shredding grinds the media into fragments so the data is physically destroyed. A quick format only clears the file table and leaves files recoverable. Reinstalling the OS or deleting user folders also leaves residual data that forensic tools can restore.",
  "A57": "Contact the user to verify satisfaction, because the urgent LCD repair is done and the laptop is already back in the field. Follow-up after a closed ticket confirms the screen still works and catches leftover problems before they escalate, which matters when the user left immediately on a trip. Ordering a spare display, updating a login script, or testing the old panel does not confirm the new screen works for the traveler.",
  "A58": "Update the anti-virus signatures next, because System Restore has already been disabled and the old restore points are gone. In the malware-removal process, the engine and definitions are updated next so the scanner can recognize current variants before it cleans the PC. Educating the user is the last step, not the one after disabling restore. Nightly scan schedules come later, and booting from original installation media is a repair or reinstall action, not this next malware task.",
  "A59": "SSH is the best way to manage a switch and a firewall in the local data center. Secure Shell is an encrypted command-line session, usually on TCP port 22, and it is the modern replacement for Telnet on network gear. RDP and VNC are graphical remote-desktop tools for computers, not typical switch or firewall consoles. A VPN can carry traffic to a remote site, but it is not the device-management protocol, and this equipment is already local.",
  "A60": "Run an anti-malware scan on the smartphone, because the browser security warnings are fake. Malicious pop-ups imitate real system alerts to push fake updates that install malware, so the phone should be scanned and the prompt should not be clicked. Airplane mode only cuts radios for a while. A VPN does not remove malware already on the device. Unpairing Bluetooth devices does not stop a browser-based scare warning.",
  "A61": "Modify the BIOS boot order so a leftover USB flash drive cannot be tried before the internal disk. Firmware walks the boot list in order; if a USB stick is first and has no valid operating system, the PC reports a missing OS even though RAM, CPU, storage, and power all passed diagnostics. A login script, OS patches, and SFC cannot change which device the firmware tries first.",
  "A62": "Adware is the most likely cause of random desktop alerts about third-party products and services. Adware injects unwanted advertisements and is often bundled with other software installs. An on-path attack intercepts network traffic, a corrupted email database would affect mail, and a failed OS update would produce Windows update errors rather than product ads.",
  "A63": "A .bat file is where an administrator would expect the Windows command cd c:\\source. Batch files run sequential cmd.exe commands such as echo, copy, del, and cd, a style that dates back to DOS. A .sh file is a Unix or Linux shell script and uses different path syntax. A .vbs file is VBScript, and a .py file is Python, neither of which uses that cmd-style cd line as native syntax.",
  "A64": "sfc is the tool that restores missing core Windows files after malware has been removed. System File Checker, usually run elevated as sfc /scannow, replaces incorrect or missing system files with known-good Microsoft copies from the component store. gpupdate refreshes Group Policy, winver only shows the Windows version, and diskpart manages disks and partitions. None of those repair OS binaries.",
  "A65": "Retrieve and securely store the computer, because sharing copyrighted material makes the PC potential evidence. Chain of custody requires taking control of the hardware, documenting who handles it, and preventing tampering so the data can still be used later. A firewall block or hashing files might support an investigation, but changing or leaving the live system is not the first evidence step. Listing licenses also does not secure the machine.",
  "A66": "Group Policy is the way to require password complexity for all Active Directory users. Domain Group Policy Objects centrally enforce security settings, including password length and complexity, across users and computers. A login script runs at sign-in but is not the password-policy mechanism. Folder redirection moves user folders, and port security controls network ports, not AD password rules.",
  "A67": "A dollar sign at the end of the share name hides the folder from browse lists. In Windows, a name such as Files$ is a hidden or administrative share; users who know the UNC path can still connect if they have rights, but the share does not appear when they browse the network. A period, exclamation mark, or hash sign does not create a hidden Windows share.",
  "A68": "An incorrect Wi-Fi password is the most likely reason the iOS phone lists networks but never shows a connection. SSIDs in the list mean the radio is on and seeing access points, and airplane mode is already off, so the join is failing on authentication. A missing cellular data plan does not affect Wi-Fi. The wireless LAN is not disabled if names appear. Bluetooth does not typically block Wi-Fi here, and a disabled Wi-Fi radio would hide the network list.",
  "A69": "An anti-static strap should be worn while upgrading the video adapter. An ESD wrist strap equalizes potential between the technician and the chassis so a static discharge does not damage the new card or motherboard, and it should clip to the case or a proper ESD ground rather than building electrical ground. A tone generator traces cables, safety goggles protect eyes, and a toner vacuum cleans printer toner. None of those apply to a graphics-card swap.",
  "A70": "Asset tags are the best way to identify PCs that have been returned for service or moved. Barcode, QR, or RFID labels tie each physical system to an inventory record for checkout, repair, and location tracking. Cable labels mark wires, topology diagrams show the network layout, and login names identify people rather than hardware.",
  "A71": "Hide extensions for known file types is the Windows option that let a virus executable look like a spreadsheet. With extensions hidden, a name such as report.xlsx.exe can appear as report.xlsx, so the user opens malware thinking it is a workbook. Showing icons instead of thumbnails, showing the full path, or always showing menus does not conceal the real file type.",
  "A72": "Multi-factor authentication and WPA3 together give the strongest office wireless encryption plus an extra login check. WPA3 is the current Wi-Fi standard with stronger key exchange and AES-based protection than WPA2, and MFA requires a second factor beyond a password. RADIUS, TACACS, and Kerberos can support authentication but are not the wireless encryption standard named here. TKIP is older WPA encryption, and UPnP opens ports automatically rather than hardening the WLAN.",
  "A73": "End-user acceptance is the last step in this firewall upgrade. After the project description, topology, risks, detailed plan, backout path, and change request are done and the work is complete, users test the result and sign off so the change record can close. A detailed upgrade plan, a backout plan, and the change-control application all happen before or during the change, not as the final close-out.",
  "A74": "gpupdate is the command that applies the new ten-character password policy on the lab laptop. Windows clients cache Group Policy and may keep using the old setting until the policy is refreshed with gpupdate or gpupdate /force. net use maps network drives, sfc repairs system files, and tasklist lists running processes. None of those enforce a domain password-length rule.",
  "A75": "Disable System Restore before removal was the missed step that let the malware return after class. Infected restore points can reinfect a cleaned PC if System Protection is left on, so the process turns protection off and deletes old points before cleanup. The technician updated anti-virus, removed malware, and made a backup, but leftover restore data could bring the infection back. Booting to WinPE, identifying symptoms, and flashing the BIOS are not the step that prevents this reinfection.",
  "A76": "A UPS is the best way to keep each server up for at least fifteen minutes during a power failure. An uninterruptible power supply rides through outages on battery and also helps with brownouts and surges, which matches a short, timed runtime. Cloud storage does not power the server. Redundant power supplies need two live feeds and provide no battery time. A surge suppressor only clips spikes and supplies no backup power.",
  "A77": "The laptops are most likely using a Windows N edition, which ships without multimedia utilities. N editions were created for Europe after antitrust rulings and omit Windows Media Player and related media features, though those features can be added later. Video playback is not a typical BIOS toggle, laptop hardware can play video, and an unrecognized file format would affect one type of file rather than omit the OS media tools entirely.",
  "A78": "Disk Management is the utility that shows a graphical summary of disks and volumes when adding a drive and extending a volume. diskmgmt.msc is built to view, format, partition, and extend storage. Performance Monitor graphs counters, Event Viewer shows logs, Task Scheduler runs jobs, and Device Manager lists hardware. None of those manage volume layout.",
  "A79": "Disable notifications while specific applications are running so browser, PDF, Store, and other toasts stop interrupting presentations. Windows Settings under System includes Notifications and Focus Assist, which can suppress alerts when a chosen app such as a slide deck is in the foreground. Switching laptops or running the presentation as Administrator does not silence those toasts. Airplane mode would cut radios and is a blunt workaround.",
  "A80": "A remote network installation is the most efficient way to upgrade twenty training-room PCs. Installing from a local server, share, or network image lets many systems be upgraded without carrying a USB stick to each desk. A recovery partition restores a factory image, a repair installation fixes an existing Windows setup, and a USB key works but does not scale well to twenty machines.",
  "A81": "Educate the end user next, because the malware is gone, scans are scheduled, and a new restore point already exists. User-awareness training on phishing and unsafe browsing is the last stage of malware removal and is what keeps the same lure from succeeding again. Disabling System Restore and updating signatures belong earlier in the process. Quarantine is an isolation step at the beginning, not after cleanup.",
  "A82": "IAM best describes setting access controls, managing authentication, and tracking who uses network resources. Identity and access management is the framework of policies and technologies that decides who can reach which systems and records that access. PAM focuses on privileged accounts, DLP prevents data leaks, and MDM manages mobile devices. Each of those is narrower than this whole job.",
  "A83": "Restore the workstation from a known-good backup, because payroll-site access failed and the browser is throwing many pop-ups. Persistent pop-ups despite modern blockers usually mean malware, and a clean backup returns the PC to a trusted state. Uninstalling the browser or using Safe Mode may not remove a system-wide infection. Proxy settings would not typically create random pop-ups.",
  "A84": "Restrict log-in times for the partner account, because that user only needs the server in the early morning. Limiting authentication to those hours blocks after-hours use even though the firewall already limits the connection to one host. Removing the server from the domain, requiring 64-bit apps, and a weekly anti-virus scan do not control when the partner can sign in.",
  "A85": "sudo gives the Linux administrator the elevated rights needed to upgrade the web server software. Superuser Do runs allowed commands with root-level permissions, which package installs require. chmod only changes file mode bits, apt is the package manager but still needs elevation to change system software, and dig only queries DNS.",
  "A86": "Power Options can keep the laptop running when the lid is closed on an external monitor and keyboard. In Power Options you set the lid-close action to Do nothing instead of Sleep. Device Manager, Personalization, and User Accounts do not control what happens when the lid shuts.",
  "A87": "IP filtering is the setting that allows wireless access for the office PCs but excludes the lobby kiosk. Filtering can deny a specific address or allow only approved ranges so that one client is blocked. SSID suppression hides the network name but does not stop a device that already knows it. Content filtering is for web categories, secure management access protects the admin interface, and a DHCP reservation assigns an address rather than denying access.",
  "A88": "Firewall filtering is the most likely reason the conference-room PC missed updates and then could not join the corporate network. A host or network firewall that blocks Windows Update or required domain ports leaves the system unpatched and can also block normal corporate connectivity. A BIOS administrator password, nobody being logged in, and disabled Guest accounts do not stop Windows Update or domain access by themselves.",
  "A89": "Data-at-rest encryption is the best protection if a field laptop is stolen or lost. Full-disk encryption such as BitLocker encrypts the entire drive so someone who removes the disk cannot read the files. Multifactor authentication, strong passwords, and device certificates protect logons, but they do not stop offline access if the drive is pulled.",
  "A90": "A supply chain attack best describes a vulnerability inserted at the software vendor and then shipped to every customer who upgraded. The attacker compromises a third-party product or dependency so the payload arrives through a trusted update. An insider threat is someone inside this company, business email compromise is a fraudulent mail scam, and an on-path attack intercepts traffic between two parties. None of those match a vendor-origin flaw in an upgrade.",
  "B6": "Perform a regular format is the correct action because the older laptop is being reused and the previous user's data must be unrecoverable. A regular format overwrites every sector with zeros, which has been the default full-format behavior since Windows Vista and stops ordinary recovery tools from reading the old files. The drive remains a usable volume afterward, so the new user can still work from it. Defragmentation only rearranges existing data, joining a Windows domain changes logon, not disk contents, and deleting the Users folder leaves the old files on disk where they can still be recovered.",
  "B7": "Take the cartridges to a hazardous waste facility, because used toner is treated as a regulated waste and must not go out with ordinary trash. Manufacturer return programs and office-supply trade-ins are acceptable recycle paths, and SDS guidance says toner is a fine particulate that is not standard refuse. Sending a bulk load to the landfill or breaking the cartridges apart and tossing the pieces treats them as everyday garbage. Contracting an incinerator is also the wrong disposal channel for leftover toner.",
  "B8": "net use is the command that maps a remote shared folder to a local drive letter so the spreadsheet appears inside File Explorer. The typical syntax is net use Z: \\\\Server\\Share, which is the command-line equivalent of Map network drive on This PC. tasklist only lists running processes, diskpart manages disks and partitions, and netstat reports network connections and sockets. None of those three create a persistent drive letter to a share.",
  "B9": "Time Machine is the macOS backup feature that can restore data from many points across the last thirty days. It keeps hourly snapshots for a day, daily backups for a month, and weekly backups after that, which matches a point-in-time recovery requirement on a Mac server. Backup and Restore is a Windows tool, Spotlight is the macOS search index, and Spaces only manages virtual desktops. Those options do not provide a thirty-day recovery timeline on macOS.",
  "B10": "An ESD strap is worn to prevent damage from static electricity while handling components. The wrist strap equalizes electrical potential between the technician and the chassis so a discharge does not punch through sensitive circuits. It should clip to the equipment chassis or a proper ESD common ground on the bench, not to building electrical ground. The strap does not cool parts, keep them dry, or keep dust off the board.",
  "B11": "/Applications is the macOS folder where installed programs live and is therefore the first place to look when uninstalling a video editor. Finder shows that root-level Applications directory to every user on the Mac. Program Files is a Windows path, /Library holds support files and preferences rather than the app bundle itself, and /Users contains home folders. Removing the utility starts in Applications, not in those other locations.",
  "B12": "Contact the end user and tell them the motherboard will not arrive until tomorrow, because the executive's laptop cannot be repaired on the promised day. Professional practice is to update the customer as soon as a timeline changes instead of waiting for them to ask. Sliding the job to the next business day or filling the open slot with another repair does not warn the person who is without a working laptop. Asking the shipper for a refund also does nothing to manage the user's expectation.",
  "B13": "dir is the Windows command that lists folder contents and can filter by extension, so a search for log files will surface them among more than a thousand items. That listing is exactly what the administrator needs inside the application folder. sfc verifies protected Windows system files, diskpart manages partitions, and robocopy copies or mirrors trees. Those tools do not inventory files by name the way dir does.",
  "B14": "Run a network speed check next, because the app's database download normally finishes in seconds and is now taking minutes. That change is a throughput symptom, so the technician should measure the current path before changing unrelated settings. Turning off Bluetooth, charging the battery, or checking cloud storage usage does not explain a slow download of the same database. If the speed test is poor, the problem is the network; if it is fine, the technician can then look at the app or server.",
  "B15": "Device Manager is the tool that can disable a USB controller while the flash drive is driving the CPU to 100 percent. It is the graphical console for viewing, enabling, disabling, and troubleshooting hardware and drivers, including USB host controllers. Services starts and stops background programs, Performance Monitor graphs counters, and Event Viewer reads logs. None of those three turn a USB controller off.",
  "B16": "Power cycle the phone first, because several work apps are crashing on launch and a restart is the least invasive next step. Mobile troubleshooting starts by force-closing or rebooting so a stuck process or bad memory state can clear before you change software. Rolling apps back, plugging the phone into power, or disabling GPS does not address a launch crash that is not tied to battery or location. If the apps still fail after the reboot, then updates, reinstalls, or logs become the next moves.",
  "B17": "Maintain a chain of custody, because legal staff will treat the exploited server as evidence and must prove it was not altered after it was taken offline. Every person who touches the hardware or the files is logged with date, time, and identity, and digital copies are often hashed so tampering is obvious. A broken chain can make the data inadmissible. Reporting the storage location, gathering old tickets, or photographing the rack are useful notes, but they do not by themselves prove integrity from seizure through testing.",
  "B18": "Educate the end-user was the missed step, because the same style of malware returned two weeks after a cleanup. User-awareness training on phishing and unsafe browsing is the last stage of malware removal and is what stops the person from clicking the same lure again. Restarting, turning System Protection back on, and quarantining the PC are earlier cleanup steps. Those actions can remove an infection once, but they do not change the behavior that brought it back.",
  "B19": "Domain access is the feature in Windows 11 Pro that Home does not include. A domain joins the PC to centralized accounts and Group Policy on a domain controller, which Home cannot do. Windows 11 is 64-bit only in both editions, the Remote Desktop client exists on Home, and a workgroup is the default peer-to-peer model Home already uses. Those items are not Pro-only capabilities.",
  "B20": "Full disk encryption is the best control if a research laptop is lost or stolen, because it protects the data at rest on the drive. BitLocker-style FDE encrypts the entire volume so removing the disk and reading it elsewhere does not expose patient or research files. A BIOS password, authenticator app, or biometric login can be bypassed by pulling the drive. A cable lock may deter casual theft, but it does not protect the files once the laptop is gone.",
  "B21": "RADIUS is the service that can check VPN users against their Windows Active Directory accounts. It is an AAA protocol used to centralize authentication for VPNs, enterprise wireless, and network gear, so one set of directory credentials works everywhere. WPA3, TKIP, and AES are wireless encryption methods, not directory authentication. They do not pass a remote user's logon to Active Directory.",
  "B22": "A cloud backup service is the only option that can take several copies a day while the smartphone is on the road. Offsite or cloud backups remain reachable over the internet, which matches a traveler who almost never comes to the office. An external USB drive, a laptop, or a nightly incremental job all require the phone to be docked to local hardware. Those onsite methods fail as soon as the user is away from that gear.",
  "B23": "An anti-static bag is what should carry the SSD between laptops so a static discharge does not damage the controller or NAND. Static-shielding bags form a Faraday cage around a bare drive during transport or storage. A padded envelope, a foam-filled box, or a cloth wrap may cushion a drop, but they do not bleed off ESD. Physical padding alone is not enough for an exposed solid-state drive.",
  "B24": "Run a malware removal utility, because Google searches on this PC are being redirected to another site's ads while the same search is clean on a second computer. Browser or system malware commonly hijacks results to push ads or more malware, and an anti-malware scan or wipe is the fix. Safe Mode, OS patches, or a different Windows profile may hide the symptom for a session. They do not remove the infection that is rewriting this machine's search results.",
  "B25": "Verify the home network configuration next, because the phone reaches sites and mail at the office and fails only at home. That location split points to Wi-Fi credentials, AP settings, or the home path rather than a broken handset. OS updates, charging, or a reboot after arriving home would not explain why the same phone works on the corporate network. Confirm the home SSID, password, and connectivity before changing the device.",
  "B26": "A clean install is the right rebuild for a web server that has a rootkit, because the disk must be wiped and the operating system laid down again. A rootkit hides in the OS or boot path, so leftover system files cannot be trusted. An in-place upgrade or a repair installation keeps the existing volume and can leave the rootkit behind. A remote network install might deliver bits, but it is not the wipe-and-reinstall method this infection requires.",
  "B27": "Place a firewall between the customer Wi-Fi and the corporate network so guests never reach company devices. Isolated guest access keeps visitors off the primary LAN even if they can see a public SSID. A second access point without isolation can still share the same LAN, WPA3 only encrypts the company SSID, static addresses do not block joiners, and MAC filters are easy to spoof. Segmentation with a firewall is what actually keeps the two networks apart.",
  "B28": "Rename the user's profile folder and delete that profile from the registry, because a domain logon that shows a default wallpaper and an empty Documents folder is a broken or temporary profile. Windows then builds a fresh profile on the next sign-in, and files can be copied back from the renamed folder. Safe Mode, adding the account to Administrators, or upgrading the OS do not rebuild a corrupted profile. Those steps leave the damaged profile in place.",
  "B29": "The website certificate has expired, because every shipping PC fails the same third-party site over HTTPS and local storage or malware would not hit all ten systems at once. Browsers warn on expired, mismatched, or untrusted certificates, and an expired public cert explains a secure-site error that is identical on every client. Missing OS patches, unencrypted local drives, or malware infections would not all surface as the same secure-connection failure. The common point of failure is the site's certificate.",
  "B30": "Assign an expiration date to the auditor accounts so those logons die when the yearly audit ends and stay dead until the next visit. Temporary vendor or contractor accounts are meant to disable automatically on a set date. Uninstalling the audit app does not stop a logon, removing group membership may still leave a usable account, and two-factor authentication only strengthens a sign-in that should not work at all. Expiration is the control that matches a once-a-year auditor.",
  "B31": "A regular format is the method that leaves the donated computers usable while making the old data unrecoverable. It overwrites every sector with zeros, unlike a quick format that only clears the file table and leaves files recoverable. Degaussing and shredding destroy the drive so the charity cannot use it. The requirement is reuse plus sanitization, which is exactly what a regular format provides.",
  "B32": "Install a UPS so overnight rendering survives the building's occasional power loss. An uninterruptible supply rides through outages and brownouts and can shut the PC down cleanly instead of dropping it to the login screen. A surge suppressor only clips spikes and does not keep the workstation powered. Saving to an external drive or using another partition does not keep a render running when the wall power dies.",
  "B33": "defrag is the command that can speed up an older hard drive that has slowed as files and apps accumulated. The Defragment and Optimize Drives tool rewrites scattered HDD clusters into contiguous layout so reads take fewer seeks. format would wipe the volume, sfc only repairs system files, xcopy copies data, and winver just shows the Windows version. Those commands do not defragment a spinning disk.",
  "B34": "Run an anti-malware utility, because the \"Access to this PC has been blocked\" banner is scareware, not the company's real security software. Fake antivirus pop-ups claim the PC is locked or infected and try to push a phone number or a payment. OS updates, checking a web server certificate, or a simple restart do not remove that hoax software. Cleaning it with anti-malware, or wiping and restoring, is the next real step.",
  "B35": "Modify the default permissions, because the Trojan used overly broad rights to delete share data and copy user documents off-site. Least privilege and group-based NTFS or share rights limit how far a compromised process can walk once it is running. Multi-factor authentication, guest-account status, full-disk encryption, password complexity, and a screensaver lock do not shrink what an already-signed-in Trojan can do on open shares. Tightening the defaults reduces the blast radius of the next infection.",
  "B36": "Remote network installation is the efficient way to push one lab image to every test PC. Image deployment clones the same build over the network and can be automated, which is the point of a shared lab image. A clean install or a bootable USB would rebuild machines one at a time from media. A repair installation only fixes an existing Windows setup and does not roll out a master image.",
  "B37": "Deny is the share permission that wins when access is assigned on a mapped drive. If a user or group is both allowed and denied, the deny entry blocks the connection, which is why deny is treated as highest priority. Allow, Full control, and List folder contents can all be overridden by a matching deny. Share permissions apply to network users at the folder, so a deny on that share stops the mapped-drive path.",
  "B38": "Electrical ground is the protection that keeps a server power fault from shocking staff. A bonded chassis and rack give fault current a path to ground instead of through a person, and ground prongs on power cords must stay in place. A battery backup keeps systems running, an air-filter mask is for particulates, and an ESD mat only equalizes static for components. Those items do not redirect dangerous fault current away from employees.",
  "B39": "This call is social engineering, specifically voice phishing, because someone posing as the help desk is fishing for location, employee ID, and passwords. Attackers use the phone to impersonate a trusted group and talk the victim into handing over secrets. A denial of service knocks a service offline, brute force guesses passwords against a logon prompt, and shoulder surfing is watching the screen in person. None of those match a scripted help-desk phone scam.",
  "B40": "Enable System Restore next, because malware is already gone and nightly signature updates and scans are already scheduled. After the PC is confirmed clean, System Protection is turned back on and a fresh restore point is created so later rollbacks are not infected. Teaching the user comes after that baseline, and quarantine or Safe Mode belong to earlier isolation and remediation. Re-enabling restore too soon would just snapshot malware, which is why it sits at this point in the process.",
  "B41": "EFS is the way to encrypt only that small set of files in the shared folder. Encrypting File System works on NTFS at the file or folder level from Properties, Advanced, Encrypt contents, and it uses the user's credentials as the key. BitLocker encrypts the whole volume, so it is too broad for a few files. Administrator rights and a dollar sign on the name do not encrypt anything.",
  "B42": "MBR is the partition style that caps a Windows volume at 2 TB. A Master Boot Record disk also allows only four primary partitions, with one marked active. GPT supports much larger disks and many more partitions, FAT32 is a file system rather than a partition table, and APFS is Apple's volume format. Those choices do not impose the 2 TB Windows MBR limit.",
  "B43": "Configure the PC for multiboot so the engineer can choose Windows or Linux at startup and run each set of apps natively. Each operating system needs its own partition or disk with the matching file system, then a boot menu presents both. Issuing two computers or booting from a pair of USB sticks is workable but wasteful, and trying to compile Linux programs under Windows will not run most Linux applications. Multiboot is the standard one-PC answer on the exam.",
  "B44": "The knowledge base is the best place to find how that email server was built so it can be reconstructed. Good articles store symptoms, build steps, and last-verified details that the ticketing system can reopen after a crash. A compliance report, an acceptable use policy, and a network topology map do not list the mail server's install and configuration steps. Those documents answer other questions and will not rebuild the box.",
  "B45": "This is a zero trust policy, because wired desktops are allowed straight to apps and data while wireless and conference-room users must authenticate through a VPN first. Zero trust assumes no path is safe just because it is on company property, so every session is verified with extra controls such as VPN, MFA, and segmentation. Multifactor authentication, Group Policy, and least privilege are related ideas, but they do not describe trusting the office LAN and challenging every other access path. The extra VPN gate is the zero-trust control in this scenario.",
  "B46": "The laptop has a malware infection, which explains the blue screens, constant high CPU, half-loaded websites, and random pop-ups together. Malware commonly burns CPU, injects ads, and breaks browsing while also destabilizing Windows. A dying drive, a bad proxy, a missing OS patch, or a video fault might cause one of those symptoms. None of those single faults produce this whole set at once.",
  "B47": "A faulty OS upgrade is the likely cause, because the iPad started rebooting at random after the system was upgraded three days ago and it was stable the week before. Random restart troubleshooting looks at recent OS or app updates first when the timeline matches. An invalid certificate, malware, a weak battery, or a bad network setting would not suddenly appear in lockstep with that upgrade. Roll back or reapply the iOS update before replacing hardware.",
  "B48": "File Explorer Options is what reveals the hidden Windows\\Installer folder. On the View tab, show hidden files, folders, and drives so system directories that Windows conceals become visible. Safe Mode, User Accounts, and Internet Options do not change hidden-file display. Until that View setting is changed, the folder will stay missing from the listing.",
  "B49": "mv is the Linux command that renames a file as well as moves it. The same utility can send a notes file into another directory or change the file's name in place. rm deletes, mkdir creates a directory, and pwd only prints the working directory. Those commands cannot rename the log file.",
  "B50": "Disconnect the laptop from the network next, because slow boots and pop-ups during Office apps are malware signs and the infected system must be quarantined. Pulling Ethernet and turning off Wi-Fi or Bluetooth stops the threat from spreading to other machines. Teaching the user, scheduling antivirus, and turning System Protection back on come later in the malware process. Isolation is the immediate step once those symptoms are recognized.",
  "B51": "Reboot the computer is the short-term fix because this laptop is already near 100 percent RAM, so applications cannot start and others crash under memory pressure. A restart ends every running process and clears the working set, which immediately frees RAM so Windows can run again. Windows warns when memory is nearly full, and apps may freeze, crash, or refuse to open while that pressure lasts. Disabling startup items can reduce what loads after the next login, but it is a longer-term change rather than a quick recovery. OS patches do not free memory that is already in use, and defragmenting the hard drive does not add RAM or stop a memory-bound crash loop.",
  "B52": "RDP is the best choice when a help desk technician needs to view and control a remote Windows desktop. Remote Desktop Protocol is Microsoft's built-in method for a full interactive GUI session and typically listens on TCP port 3389. A VPN only builds an encrypted path onto the network; it does not by itself show or drive the other user's screen. VNC can remote a desktop on mixed platforms, but it is not the native or preferred Windows tool in this scenario. SSH is a command-line remote shell and does not provide a graphical desktop.",
  "B53": "Turn off Fast Startup so a full shutdown actually occurs and the UEFI firmware can present its setup hotkey on the next power-on. Fast Startup is a hybrid boot that writes the Windows kernel session to disk during shutdown to speed the next start, which often skips the firmware key prompt after what looks like a shutdown. File Explorer Options and Indexing Options only change how Windows searches and shows files, not how the PC powers off. Safe Mode still boots Windows rather than entering firmware setup. Ease of Access settings do not restore the BIOS configuration key.",
  "B54": "Run an anti-virus scan because an unattended mouse that opens applications and changes Control Panel settings is a classic sign of remote-control malware or spyware. Spyware secretly monitors activity and can take over input to gather data or alter the system without consent. Cycling the firewall does not remove software that is already running on the PC. Uninstalling recently added applications may miss a hidden payload or a malicious update. OS patches harden the system going forward but do not clean an infection that is already moving the pointer.",
  "B55": "Change board approval is what is blocking the weekend upgrade. The administrator already wrote the scope, risk assessment, and change plan, but a normal change still needs the Change Control Board to review risk, priority, and scheduling before anyone implements it. A Friday-afternoon risk write-up leaves no time for that authorization, so the work cannot proceed over the weekend. The scenario does not mention missing upgrade files, a shortage of staff, or a job that is too long to finish. Those operational issues are not what change-management policy is waiting on here.",
  "B56": "The date and time on the laptop is incorrect, which explains HTTPS security alerts on every secure site while the same sites work on the smartphone. Browsers check certificate validity against the local clock, so time drift makes trusted certificates look expired or not yet valid and triggers warnings on all HTTPS pages. The phone is a different device with its own clock, so it does not see the error. A wrong subnet mask would break general connectivity, not generate certificate warnings. Anti-virus signatures and the phone's OS version do not control how the laptop validates website certificates.",
  "B57": "Disable Bluetooth and cellular connections and close apps which work in the background to stretch the tablet's battery beyond a few hours. Unused radios and leftover background apps keep the radio and CPU awake and drain charge quickly, so turning those radios off and closing the apps reduces power use. Installing OS patches does not immediately fix short runtime on a new tablet. Raising brightness and staying on a VPN both increase draw. A soft reset may briefly help but does not change the settings that are wasting battery.",
  "B58": "Zero-touch deployment is the method that can join the corporate domain and apply each user's email settings without a technician walking through setup. It is an automated install that applies company-specific configuration so a device can be powered on and become ready with no domain or account prompts. A recovery partition only restores that machine to a factory or captured state. A standard image still usually needs post-image domain and mail work. An in-place upgrade updates an existing OS and does not provision new machines this way.",
  "B59": "Verify the requirements for the new application first, because the old tax software ran well and only the newer version is slow. A first troubleshooting step is to confirm the workstation meets the new program's CPU, RAM, disk, and OS needs before changing or repairing anything. Rolling back might restore speed later, but it skips finding out whether this PC is undersized for the update. A repair install assumes the files are damaged, which is not suggested yet. A system file check looks at Windows system files, not whether a third-party tax app is too heavy for the hardware.",
  "B60": "FileVault is the macOS feature that encrypts the entire system drive so data stays protected if the laptop is lost or stolen. It is built-in full disk encryption using AES, tied to the user's credentials or a recovery key, and is turned on in System Settings under Privacy and Security. Spaces and Mission Control only arrange desktops and windows. Keychain stores passwords and certificates; it does not encrypt the whole disk.",
  "B61": "Key fobs and a palmprint scanner together provide multi-factor authentication on the new data center door. A key fob is a possession factor, typically a contactless RFID token on a keychain, and a palmprint scanner is a biometric inherence factor, so both something you have and something you are are required. Cabinet locks, USB locks, and cable locks secure equipment, not the room door. A privacy filter only hides a screen from onlookers and is not an authentication factor.",
  "B62": "A TOTP app is the lowest-cost secure factor for field technicians who already carry phones. An authenticator application generates time-based one-time passwords in software, so the company does not have to buy retina scanners or hardware tokens for every traveler. A retina scanner is expensive specialized hardware. A key fob is a physical token with per-device cost. A magnetometer is a compass-style sensor and is not an authentication method.",
  "B63": "Schedule periodic anti-virus scans is the next malware-removal step after the infection has already been identified and removed. Once remediation is done, you automate defense with scheduled antivirus scans and signature updates, and you keep Windows Update current so the system does not get reinfected. Disconnecting from the network and disabling System Restore belong earlier, during quarantine and before cleanup. End-user education is the last step, after protections are back in place and scanning is scheduled.",
  "B64": "Configure an NTP server so the executive laptop continuously corrects the clock instead of slowly falling minutes behind each week. Time drift happens when hardware timekeeping is unreliable, and NTP keeps Windows aligned to an accurate network time source, which this assembly-line monitoring job needs. A login script that stamps the time is a crude workaround and only runs at sign-in. Changing the time zone does not stop the clock from losing time. Replacing laptop batteries is a hardware guess and is not the first operational fix for a few minutes of weekly drift.",
  "B65": "A biometric lock and an access control vestibule are the two controls that actually secure the data center door. The vestibule, or mantrap, uses two doors so only one can be open or unlocked at a time, and biometrics confirm the person who is entering. An ACL filters network traffic, not people at a door. Bollards stop vehicles outside. Extra lighting and a motion sensor help detect or deter activity but do not authenticate or gate entry the way a lock and vestibule do.",
  "B66": "Check the Event Viewer logs next, because the same startup error remains after uninstalling and reinstalling the application. Event Viewer collects application, system, and security events that often name a missing dependency, a service failure, or a crash code the installer did not show. Performance Monitor is for utilization trends, not a recurring launch error. Device Manager is for hardware devices. Disabling random background services is guesswork until the log points to a cause.",
  "B67": "Spear phishing best describes these fake invoice emails sent to four accounting users with details that look real but use invalid invoice numbers. Spear phishing is targeted social engineering aimed at a specific group, using plausible business context to push a malicious payment link. Broad phishing blasts are less tailored than this accounting-focused lure. A denial of service attacks availability, not inboxes. Shoulder surfing is watching a screen in person. An evil twin is a rogue wireless access point.",
  "B68": "Call the customer and ask for more information because a note that only says the laptop doesn't work is not enough to start repairs. Good communication practice is to ask probing open-ended questions, take detailed notes, and restate the issue so you understand the scope before you touch the machine. Starting the laptop, replacing the power adapter, or booting a diagnostics disc are technical steps that come after you know what actually failed. Acting first on a one-line ticket often fixes the wrong thing or creates new problems.",
  "B69": "Linux is the free, open-source operating system in this list. It is a Unix-like family with many distributions such as Ubuntu, Debian, Red Hat, and Fedora, and it can run on a wide range of hardware without a license fee. macOS, Windows, and iOS are commercial, closed-source platforms that require vendor licensing. Linux's usual exam tradeoff is weaker laptop driver support and fewer official support options than those commercial systems, but that does not change that it is the FOSS answer here.",
  "B70": "Create a file-level backup each day so users can roll a document or spreadsheet back to any daily copy from the last month. File-level backups capture user data on a schedule that matches version history, which is what this recovery option requires. A monthly image is too coarse to restore yesterday's spreadsheet. Off-site tape storage is for disaster recovery, not self-service daily versions. Handing out USB flash drives is not a managed backup and is easy to lose or skip.",
  "B71": "Contact the company's security officer next, because repeated visits to a site that violates the AUP are a policy matter, not a first-line firewall or password change. An acceptable use policy states what employees may do with company internet and devices, and violations are handled through the security or HR process, often with disciplinary action. Blocking the site, scanning every PC, or resetting that user's password are technical actions that skip the required reporting path and may destroy evidence or exceed the administrator's authority.",
  "B72": "The vbs extension is the script type commonly used inside Microsoft Office. VBScript drives classic Office macros and other Windows scripting through Windows Script Host, even though PowerShell has largely replaced it elsewhere. Python scripts are not the built-in Office macro language. A bat file is a Windows command-prompt batch script. JavaScript is used in browsers and some hosts, but it is not the usual Office macro extension on the A+ exam.",
  "B73": "A workgroup is the right model for five SOHO Windows PCs that only need to share documents with each other. A workgroup is a small peer-to-peer group with local accounts and manual share permissions, which matches this office size. A domain needs Active Directory and centralized controllers, which is more than a five-PC shop requires. A proxy server filters or caches web traffic; it does not share files. Remote Desktop controls a screen; it does not create a file-sharing group.",
  "B74": "Cloud storage is the most likely leak path when product photos taken on a tablet appeared on a rumor site a week later. Tablets commonly auto-upload pictures to iCloud, Google Photos, or similar accounts, so a synced or shared album can expose images without anyone copying them by hand. USB flash drive use is less typical as the default path off a tablet. Application updates do not publish photos. Deleted email is unrelated to camera pictures leaving the device.",
  "B75": "Credit card transactions are the regulated data type that matters most for a United States site that sells monthly subscriptions. Those charges are cardholder data under PCI DSS, which requires protecting, segmenting, encrypting, and auditing payment information. Government-issued ID is personal data but is not the core of a subscription checkout. Healthcare data falls under HIPAA and is not implied here. Software license terms are contracts, not a regulated data class like payment cards.",
  "B76": "A VPN should be configured so messages stay private on the conference wireless network. A virtual private network encrypts traffic from the phone to a trusted concentrator, so people on the same public Wi-Fi cannot read the session. A strong password and multi-factor authentication protect the account login, not the packets on the event SSID. A network-based firewall sits on a perimeter device and is not something you enable on the phone for this trip.",
  "B77": "Perform a hardware diagnostic next, because random reboots started after the RAM upgrade from 8 GB to 16 GB. System instability with no single app to blame often means the new modules are loose, incompatible, or faulty, and a memory test will confirm that before you change software. Windows Update does not fix a bad DIMM. A BIOS update might help compatibility later, but it is not the first step. Replacing the storage drive ignores the hardware that just changed.",
  "B78": "Perform occasional recovery tests to prove the nightly VM-to-tape jobs can actually rebuild a server. Regular restore tests check integrity and expose media or job failures before a real outage; an untested backup is only an assumption. Sending tapes off-site protects against a building loss but does not prove the data is readable. A battery backup keeps the drives powered; it does not validate the backup set. Extra file-level backups are a different method, not confirmation that these snapshots restore.",
  "B79": "System in Control Panel is where you enable inbound Remote Desktop for the help desk. The System applet exposes computer name, domain or workgroup membership, System Protection, and the Remote settings that allow RDP connections. Internet Options controls browser and proxy behavior. Devices and Printers manages peripherals. Network and Sharing Center handles adapters and file sharing, not the Remote Desktop listener.",
  "B80": "Enable data usage notifications so the Android phone warns the traveler before cellular use overseas turns into overage charges. Android can alert or cap data at a threshold, which is the direct control for roaming limits. A VPN encrypts traffic but still consumes cellular data. Removing high-data apps is extreme and still leaves the OS and remaining apps able to roam. Using only a cellular hotspot would continue to bill the same mobile data, not reduce it.",
  "B81": "Disconnect from all power sources first when you begin a motherboard upgrade. Unplug the cords, and on many systems press the power button briefly to drain standby power, so you are not working on a live chassis. An ESD strap is important before you touch components, but it is not the first safety action while the server may still be powered. Safety goggles matter for cutting or chemical work, not as the opening step here. Removing the CMOS battery is not required to start the upgrade and can clear firmware settings unnecessarily.",
  "B82": "Use a share drive for the installation source so the large setup package does not have to occupy the workstation's already-full local disk. Running setup from a network share leaves the installer off the system drive and still installs the video editor locally once space is available for the program itself. Compressing the installer does not create enough room for a typical editing suite. Installing the application onto a network share would make a disk-heavy editor depend on the LAN. Copying files by hand into the application folder skips the installer and still needs local space.",
  "B83": "An open-source license is the best fit for a home photo editor with no monthly subscription. Free and open-source software is typically free to use and includes source access, so the user can install a capable editor without a commercial subscription. A corporate license is for organizations and is not a home no-cost option. A personal license is often still paid or subscription-based. DRM is a copy-protection technology, not a license that gives you the application.",
  "B84": "Task Manager is the utility that shows how much RAM this leaking application is using. The Processes view lists each program's live Memory use so you can watch the working set grow until the machine is exhausted, and you can end the task if needed. Event Viewer records errors, not a live memory graph. Device Manager lists hardware. Programs and Features only uninstalls software and does not display RAM consumption.",
  "B85": "Run Startup Repair from the Advanced Boot Options to break a reboot loop that happens before the Windows login screen. That repair path fixes boot-loader and startup configuration problems when Windows cannot start normally. Defragmenting in Safe Mode does not repair a boot loop, and you may not reach Safe Mode anyway. Reinstalling from original media is a last resort after repair tools fail. Changing BIOS boot order helps when the wrong disk or USB is selected, not when Windows itself starts and then restarts.",
  "B86": "The software is a Trojan horse: a car-shopping browser add-on that triggers UAC is asking the user to grant elevated rights to code that is only pretending to be helpful. A Trojan disguises itself as legitimate software so the user executes the payload. An OS update does not make a third-party add-on request UAC. Being in a botnet already would not, by itself, explain this install prompt. A worm spreads on its own and does not need the user to approve a disguised add-on install.",
  "B87": "whoami is the command that prints the user account attached to each terminal session. With many servers and different credentials on screen, whoami shows which identity that window is using so you do not run a change in the wrong security context. winver only reports the Windows edition and build. nslookup queries DNS. net use lists mapped drives and share connections, not the logged-on user.",
  "B88": "Assign configuration profiles for each group so email and security settings can be pushed differently to the four user populations. MDM platforms apply predefined profiles for corporate mail, lock screens, encryption, and other policy, which scales better than one-off setup. Restoring from cloud backups brings back personal data, not a standard corporate image per group. Written instructions and extra support teams do not enforce or deploy the settings consistently.",
  "B89": "Performance Monitor is the tool for tracking and reporting utilization across a full day. It can graph counters in real time and use Data Collector Sets to log CPU and other metrics over 24 hours so a random 100 percent spike is captured. Device Manager does not record performance. Services only starts or stops background programs. Task Scheduler launches jobs; it does not trend system utilization.",
  "B90": "Change the router's default password so an attacker cannot log into the SOHO admin page and rewrite the default routes. Access points and home routers ship with well-known factory credentials, and anyone who still has those can take full administrative control. MAC filtering only limits which clients associate; it does not protect the admin account. Enabling WPS adds a weak join method. Disabling unused interfaces is good hardening but does not stop a login with the default password.",
  "A1": "The accepted command is sfc /scannow. System File Checker scans protected Windows files and replaces damaged or missing copies after malware may have changed them. sfc by itself does not start the scan, DISM repairs the component store that sfc draws from, and chkdsk fixes disk and file-system errors rather than OS binaries.",
  "A2": "CompTIA's malware-removal process is a required order: verify symptoms, quarantine the system (disconnect the network), disable System Restore so an infected snapshot cannot be saved, remediate (update anti-malware, then scan/remove or reimage), schedule scans and updates, enable System Restore, and educate the end user last. Updating signatures happens only after System Restore is off so a later restore cannot bring the malware back. User education is last so the same click does not reinfect a cleaned PC.",
  "A3": "FRT is facial recognition that uses the built-in camera. PII is personally identifiable information such as names and home addresses. GFS is grandfather-father-son backup rotation for monthly, weekly, and daily copies. AUP is the acceptable-use policy in the employee handbook. BEC is business email compromise — a message that appears to come from the CEO but did not. EULA is a software license and TOTP is a rotating authenticator code, so those two stay unmatched.",
  "A4": "Map the share with net use g: \\\\gate-room\\ship-diagnostics. net use assigns a drive letter to a UNC path so the folder appears in File Explorer. A trailing backslash and /persistent:yes or /persistent:no are optional. ping, nslookup, and netstat do not attach a share to a letter.",
  "B1": "Real-time CPU, memory, disk, and network use is Task Manager. Collecting the same counters over a 24-hour window is Performance Monitor. Windows and BIOS version live in System Information (msinfo32). A daily automatic update check is Task Scheduler. System Configuration is boot and startup apps, Device Manager is hardware, and Registry Editor is raw settings — none of those match these four tasks.",
  "B2": "tracert 8.8.8.8 (or pathping 8.8.8.8) lists each router hop toward the destination so you can see where the WAN path dies. ping only tests the far end, ipconfig shows the local adapter, and nslookup queries DNS. pathping also reports per-hop loss; both commands are accepted.",
  "B3": "Creating an Active Directory account and adding groups is PowerShell (.ps1). Changing a webpage from the visitor's IP is JavaScript (.js). One backup script that runs on Windows, Linux, and macOS is Python (.py). Automating Excel is VBScript (.vbs). Restarting Linux servers at night is a shell script (.sh). Batch (.bat) is Windows cmd only, so it is unused here.",
  "B4": "Windows 11 Home is limited to a workgroup. Domain join, BitLocker, and hosting Remote Desktop are Pro and Enterprise. The exam edition table lists 6 TB of RAM on Enterprise only. Home has a Remote Desktop client but cannot be the host, and it cannot join a domain.",
  "B5": "chkdsk /f repairs logical file-system errors; chkdsk /r includes /f and also looks for bad sectors. An optional drive letter such as C: is allowed. sfc repairs Windows system files, format destroys the volume, and defrag only rearranges data on a spinning disk."
};
ALL_QUESTIONS.forEach(it => { if (EXPLAIN[it.id]) it.why = EXPLAIN[it.id]; });
